{"type":"text","timestamp":1778977319098,"sessionID":"ses_1ccb0a604ffetWFnLzVrOWuran","part":{"id":"prt_e334f92d8001hAjKtnKyZSUnw5","messageID":"msg_e334f8400001SNr18S1bC6EpT7","sessionID":"ses_1ccb0a604ffetWFnLzVrOWuran","type":"text","text":"The issue is clear enough to implement: a new CL term with an agreed label, reviewed text definition, synonyms, and a proposed logical definition. I’m checking existing CL/UBERON terms in `cl-edit.owl` plus the cited papers to avoid guessing IDs or hierarchy.","time":{"start":1778977313496,"end":1778977319085}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778977319116,"sessionID":"ses_1ccb0a604ffetWFnLzVrOWuran","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_JrOJsn6yV7h5O7cSohi2HUW0","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"fallopian tube","path":"/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/src/ontology","include":"cl-edit.owl"},"output":"Found 24 matches\n/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/src/ontology/cl-edit.owl:\n  Line 28445: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1277\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001591 \"fallopian tube glandular cell\")\n\n  Line 28446: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1277\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001591 \"fallopian tube glandular cells\")\n\n  Line 31221: # Class: obo:CL_4030006 (fallopian tube secretory epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 31223: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://doi.org/10.1186/1477-7827-7-129\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://doi.org/10.3390/cells8080933\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://www.proteinatlas.org/learn/dictionary/normal/fallopian+tube\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4030006 \"An epithelial cell that is part of the fallopian tube that secretes mucus and oviduct-specific products in response to hormonal stimulation from estrogen and luteinizing hormone. This fallopian tube secretory cell is similar in height to the ciliated cell, but typically exhibits a more narrow, columnar shape. Its nucleus is ovoid and oriented perpendicular to the cell's long axis, with denser chromatin and a smaller nucleolus compared to the ciliated cell.\")\n\n  Line 31228: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ccr.2013.10.013\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://www.proteinatlas.org/learn/dictionary/normal/fallopian+tube\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4030006 \"The other cell types of the fallopian tube epithelium include the ciliated cell and the intercalary cell, or peg cell. The fallopian tube secretory epithelial cell type has been implicated as a source of high-grade serous ovarian carcinoma.\")\n\n  Line 31229: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4030006 \"fallopian tube secretory epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 31234: # Class: obo:CL_4030007 (fallopian tube multiciliated epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 31236: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16565155\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://www.proteinatlas.org/humanproteome/tissue/fallopian+tube\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4030007 \"A multi-ciliated epithelial cell that is part of the fallopian tube, mainly found on the apex of the mucosal folds. This cell exhibits a columnar shape with an oval nucleus and is characterized by the presence of cilia on its surface. The coordinated beating of these cilia, together with peristaltic contractions, contributes to the self-propulsion of spermatozoa, the transport of ovum during ovulation and the transport of the fertilized ovum to the intramural fallopian tube.\")\n\n  Line 31240: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4030007 \"fallopian tube ciliated cell\")\n\n  Line 31241: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4030007 \"fallopian tube multiciliated cell\")\n\n  Line 31243: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4030007 \"fallopian tube multiciliated epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 31973: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:2210508\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:22911892\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30023181\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033014 \"A small, narrow, peg-shaped epithelial cell with little cytoplasm that is part of oviduct epithelium. This cell is rarer than the ciliated and secretory epithelial cells of the fallopian tube epithelium and is often found intercalated between them. Peg cells are generally distributed basally along the epithelium and have been found in high concentrations at the fimbriated, distal end of the fallopian tube in humans. It may have a regenerative/stem-cell function. In humans, markers include EPCAM, CD44, and ITGA6.\")\n\n  Line 31978: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:22911892\") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4033014 \"fallopian tube epithelial stem-like cell\")\n\n  Line 33828: # Class: obo:CL_4052018 (fallopian tube epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 33830: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Wikipedia:Fallopian_tube\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052018 \"Any epithelial cell that is part of the fallopian tube.\")\n\n  Line 33835: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4052018 \"fallopian tube epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 33838: # Class: obo:CL_4052019 (fallopian tube non-ciliated epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 33840: AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052019 \"Any epithelial cell that is part of the fallopian tube and lacks cilia.\")\n\n  Line 33843: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4052019 \"fallopian tube non-ciliated cell\")\n\n  Line 33844: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4052019 \"fallopian tube non-ciliated epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 33873: # Class: obo:CL_4052022 (fallopian tube smooth muscle cell)\n\n  Line 33875: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31183831\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31613440\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052022 \"A smooth muscle cell that is part of the fallopian tube. This cell is responsible for peristaltic contractions that facilitate gamete and embryo transport, fluid mixing, and embryo admission to the uterus.\")\n\n  Line 33880: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:18621753\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4052022 \"The fallopian tube smooth muscle cell in the human contracts dynamically in response to hormones and signaling molecules. Prostaglandins, particularly PGF2α and PGE2, have been shown to increase muscular contractions, while progesterone, levonorgestrel, mifepristone, oxytocin, and human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) decrease them. These contractile responses